# Rust
/target/
# Side target directories. Two cargo runs sharing one `target/` corrupt it, so
# a parallel session uses its own `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` — and an untracked
# directory that is not *ignored* is one `cargo package` sweeps into the
# tarball, which is how the scratchpad note below came to be written.
/target-*/
**/*.rs.bk
*.pdb

# Node
node_modules/
npm-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
pnpm-debug.log*

# Published JS package staging (crates/threers-js/build.sh)
/crates/threers-js/dist/

# Wasm / web build output
/web/pkg/*
!/web/pkg/.gitignore
# …and the per-demo bundles, which `pkg/*` above does not match: each is a
# megabyte or four of built wasm, and three of them were being both committed
# and published before this line existed.
/web/pkg-*/
/web/threejs-shim.d.ts
# Regenerated by `web/build.sh` / `generate-features-addon.mjs`
/web/features.js
/web/mesh-bvh-addon.js
/web/bvh-csg-addon.js
/web/nurbs-addon.js

# Parity test artifacts (screenshots, diffs, logs)
/tests/parity/out/
/tests/parity/out-*/

# Reference material and oracles for the binary loaders — fetched C++ sources,
# a node_modules with Google's Draco decoder in it, and generated fixtures. All
# of it is reproducible and none of it is ours, but the directory is untracked
# rather than ignored, which means `cargo package` sweeps it into the tarball:
# cargo lists untracked-but-unignored files and ships them. That is how 1.5 MB
# of someone else's source nearly went to crates.io.
/scratchpad/

# Local diagnostic dumps
*.bak
*.pending-snap

# Example video export output
/out/

# Env / secrets
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# OS / editor
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.idea/
.vscode/
*.log

